#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# factor_square_free

#% text
# `factor_square_free(p)` gives the square-free factorisation of the polynomial `p`: a product of powers of square-free polynomials that have no common factor.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Each factor is square-free, but need not be irreducible. The factorisation needs only gcds of `p` and its derivatives, so it is cheaper than `factor`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
factor_square_free(x^3 - 3*x + 2)
#% output
#= (2 + x)*(-1 + x)^2

#% cell
factor_square_free((x^2 - 1)^2*(x + 3))
#% output
#= (3 + x)*(-1 + x^2)^2

#% cell
factor_square_free(x^4 + 2*x^2 + 1)
#% output
#= (1 + x^2)^2

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# Compared with `factor`, which splits each factor into irreducibles:

#% cell
p = expand((x^2 - 1)*(x - 2)^3); [factor_square_free(p), factor(p)]
#% output
#= [(-1 + x^2)*(-2 + x)^3, (-1 + x)*(1 + x)*(-2 + x)^3]

#% text
# The product gives the polynomial back:

#% cell
p = x^5 - x^4 - 2*x^3 + 2*x^2 + x - 1; expand(factor_square_free(p)) == p
#% output
#= true

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `factor`, `polynomial_gcd`, `is_square_free`, `discriminant`
